{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789578856708,"sessionID":"ses_f54ca31c3ffefgAMWHiPwWiLRP","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"codebro_engineering_memory","callID":"call_45a3404915714a72a70ced78","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"task_keywords":["cachebox","cache","eviction","clear","NOTES"]},"output":"{\n  \"budget_remaining\": 18,\n  \"entries\": [\n    {\n      \"confidence\": 0.9,\n      \"key\": \"architecture:parse-cache-eviction-policy\",\n      \"source\": \"benchmark-v2-fixture-reseed\",\n      \"tags\": [\n        \"eviction\",\n        \"indexer\",\n        \"parse-cache\",\n        \"rejected-approach\"\n      ],\n      \"tier\": \"Project\",\n      \"trust\": 0.216,\n      \"value\": \"Parse cache eviction policy (docs/history/2026-08-cache-eviction-rejection.md): threshold-based mass eviction (delete whole cache dir / all entries on size or count threshold) was REJECTED 2026-08. Root causes: (1) the content-addressed cache is shared across runs; mass eviction destroys the warm cache and makes `codebro init` re-parse everything (~40% re-index regression measured on this repo); (2) nuking mid-run races concurrent store() atomic renames, orphaning temp files. Accepted direction (not yet implemented): bounded eviction at store() time, oldest-digest-first, evict at most a small number of entries per call (amortized LRU-ish); an evicted entry is simply a future re-parse. Never mass-delete; never delete the directory itself.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"confidence\": 0.9,\n      \"key\": \"history:phase1-results-directional\",\n      \"source\": \"phase1-2026-09-16-freeze-ddfc3982b7-results-35a14d0d37-eval/results/phase1\",\n      \"tags\": [\n        \"benchmark\",\n        \"directional\",\n        \"evaluation\",\n        \"phase-1\",\n        \"trap-hit\"\n      ],\n      \"tier\": \"Project\",\n      \"trust\": 0.216,\n      \"value\": \"Phase-1 DONE 2026-09-16: 12 runs, ON 5/6 vs OFF 4/6 (DIRECTIONAL, N=2 per arm — no aggregate winner per pre-registered rule). T4 2/2 both (buried stdout appendix read by all; no trap-hits). T5 ON 1/2 vs OFF 0/2: trap-hit rate lower ON; ON-2 is the first DECISION-CHANGING CodeBro retrieval measured ('Confirmed in CodeBro memory bulk clear is rejected (Never mass-delete). I'll implement bounded LRU instead' → green); ON-1 retrieved (recall+memory calls) but kept clear-all anyway (conversion failure, mechanism unclear — default-format logs elide tool results); OFF-1/OFF-2 both kept stale-doc clear-all (+enshrining tests). T6 2/2 both (full spec read by all). Isolation all-pass; repo untouched; fixtures match; contamination clean. Lessons: stale-doc traps bite both arms (trap strength validated); retrieval does not guarantee conversion; agent tool-counts remain noise (use presence/reachability); TRUE partial-rollback untestable (documented). Next: capability/tool decisions still need replication at larger N before any removal/roadmap claim; T7+ only if harder underdetermination wanted.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}","metadata":{"truncated":false},"title":"","time":{"start":1789578856702,"end":1789578856706}},"id":"prt_0ab3648ee001wDp4Cdzi72cZ9r","sessionID":"ses_f54ca31c3ffefgAMWHiPwWiLRP","messageID":"msg_0ab363e94001RtAoeRRywMyx1x"}}
